Node: wait for a slow reader on stdout and stderr instead of failing

Node makes a pipe or socket on stdout or stderr non-blocking once it
opens process.stdout or process.stderr on it, and it does that for
every Worker it constructs, to forward the Worker's stdio. A program
with pthreads starts Workers before main(), so from then on its output
is written synchronously to a non-blocking descriptor: out() and err()
(and with them printf() on every file system, and dbg()) through
fs.writeSync() on fds 1 and 2, and NODERAWFS's writes to its standard
streams. Once the reader falls 64 KiB behind, a write either goes
through in part, and out() drops the rest, or throws EAGAIN, which
aborts the program; under NODERAWFS write() fails with EAGAIN on a
descriptor the program never made non-blocking.

Write everything out() and err() are given, and under NODERAWFS retry a
write that fails with EAGAIN unless the program set O_NONBLOCK on its
stream itself. Node can't wait for a descriptor to become writable
synchronously, so both sleep 1 ms with Atomics.wait() and try again.
This works the same on a pthread's Worker, whose writes to fds 1 and 2
reach the process's own descriptors.

